a parenthesised dereference is an lvalue.

`(*p)++` SEGFAULTED, while `p[0]++` and `*p += 1` were always correct:

    p = buf + 8;  (*p)++;

    mov_rax,[rax]              p
    movzx_rax,BYTE_PTR_[rax]   the byte -- 0
    # postfix inc/dec
    movzx_rax,BYTE_PTR_[rax]   dereference 0            SIGSEGV

The parentheses are around the DEREFERENCE, so the increment applies to what p
points at and the last dereference has to yield an ADDRESS. It loaded instead,
and the postfix handler then treated the byte's VALUE as its address.

THE ELEVENTH COPY OF THE ONE RULE. is_assignment and is_compound_operator read
the NEXT TOKEN, and for a parenthesised target that token is `)`. zzzs, zzzw
and zzzz are the same rule at three other spellings; this is the fourth.
assign_through_parens is already set by primary_expr's paren branch when an
assignment or a postfix ++/-- follows the closing paren -- it had simply never
reached this decision.

ONLY WHEN `)` IS NEXT, because the flag belongs to the whole parenthesised
expression and not to every dereference inside it. In `(a[*p]) = v` the `*p` is
a sub-expression that must still load, and the token after it is `]`. Same
guard shape as zzzs's num_dereference_after_postfix test: an lvalue applies to
the last step of a chain, never to one still to be followed. Bit 16 of case 125
covers the other half -- `**(pp)` still has to load once to reach the inner
pointer.

FOUND BY LOOKING AT THE NEIGHBOURS of case 119, and it predates all of that
work: it fails identically on the compiler from before EXPERIMENT-zzzw.

WORTH ONE LINE, BECAUSE A COMMENT CAUSED IT. cc_core.c's paren_lvalue site
names this exact spelling as one it handles -- "`(*p)++` the same thing written
the other way round". It does not. That site is the `*(...)` path, where the
star is OUTSIDE the parentheses, and it never sees this shape at all. The
comment is why nobody looked.

WHAT IT DOES NOT CLAIM. `++(*p)` -- the PREFIX spelling -- still segfaults.
prefix_lvalue is computed from the token in front of the STARS, which for that
shape is `(`, so nothing inside the parentheses can see the operator; the only
place it is still visible is the token before the open paren, which is a
different site. Attempted and reverted rather than half-moved. Case 125 stops
at the postfix and compound forms and says so.

